+<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Who pays for the CL-100 in South Carolina, the buyer or the seller?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The state regulation for the report does not say who orders it or who pays for it. The buyer and seller agree on that in the sale contract. A buyer's lender can also ask for one before it lends."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How old can a roof be for insurance in South Carolina?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"We found no public South Carolina rule on how old a roof can be for insurance. An insurer that asks for a four-point inspection gets the roof's age and its years of life left from the report. For an FHA loan, the roof needs at least two years of life left."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How long is a CL-100 good for?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A CL-100 has no expiry date in the state regulation. A buyer's lender or the sale contract can ask for a report done within a set number of days."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do I have to fix what the buyer's inspector finds?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Not under South Carolina's disclosure law. You fix what you agree to fix in the sale contract, and a buyer's loan can still require some repairs before it closes."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Are repairs before selling tax deductible?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The IRS treats painting and fixing leaks as repairs. Repairs do not go into your home's basis, what you paid plus improvements, unless they are part of a larger remodeling job. A new roof is an improvement and does. A tax professional can tell you how this applies to your sale."}}]}</script>
